WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
25% more housing starts followed flood insurance in the communities that joined it. This is Unintended

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.660
Consequences, Episode 160. How Congress tried to swap storm bailouts for premiums and local

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.400
rules. How the program actually rolled out. How cheaper coverage pulled building toward

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.480
the water. What the later rate fights ran into. And what that price signal still asks. Congress

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:29.400
passed the National Flood Insurance Act on August 1, 1968. Hurricane Betsy in 1965 had been

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.140
America's first billion dollar storm, and federal disaster relief costs were climbing.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.020
President Lyndon B. Johnson championed the bill after years of ad hoc relief.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.040
Earlier attempts under President Truman and Eisenhower had stalled.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:48.260
Betsy's damage, plus rapid coastal population growth, is what finally moved Congress.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.400
Lawmakers wanted property owners paying premiums, and towns adopting floodplain rules instead

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.400
of unpredictable taxpayer bailouts after every storm.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.700
firm. Private insurers had refused to write this coverage since the 1927 Mississippi River

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.700
flood. Millions of people had no recovery path except a congressional appropriation.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:14.120
The 1968 Act tried to fill that gap and discipline future growth at the same time. Subsidized

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.960
coverage would be available only where a community-enforced building standards meant to cut future

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.840
exposure. At the time, that looked like a rational middle path. Leave coastal residents uninsured

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.300
or keep writing open-ended federal checks.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.080
The Act tried to do neither.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.740
Tying coverage to local map adoption and elevation standards looked like a way to make future growth

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.440
pay part of its own risk, while still protecting people already there.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.480
The people who wrote it were trying to share risk and encourage safer building.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.060
The Flood Disaster Protection Act of 1973 added a harder edge.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.980
If a property sat in a special flood hazard area and carried a federally-backed mortgage,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.900
the owner had to buy the coverage.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.460
By the late 1970s, the program was operating in thousands of communities.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.340
The Federal Insurance Administration ran it first.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.380
The Federal Emergency Management Agency, FEMA, ran it later.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.020
Participation grew as maps were finished and lenders enforced the purchase rule.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.300
Proponents argued that insurance plus land use rules would gradually steer development off

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.360
the riskiest land.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.520
A few observers noted that subsidized rates might blunt that steering.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.420
The dominant view was that the regulatory half of the bargain would keep growth and check.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.140
Communities that joined had to adopt minimum elevation and setback ordinances.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.440
Those rules applied only to new construction inside the mapped floodplain.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.360
Outside those lines, or for a cash buyer, the insurance subsidy operated without the same break.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.880
A 2017 study used Florida's staggered adoption of the program in the 1970s and 1980s.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.200
National Flood Insurance Program participation, NFIP participation, raised housing starts

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:53.900
and permits.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.480
The increase showed most clearly in non-coastal counties.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.920
Coastal counties showed little or no net gain once floodplain management rules were enforced.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.560
Even so, subsidized premiums cut the private cost of building near water.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.680
Over decades, more capital flowed into shoreline counties that were already growing fast.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:18.060
Florida's population rose 579% between 1950 and 2010.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.720
Texas grew 226% in the same years.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.520
By 2010, 39% of Americans lived in coastal shoreline counties.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.280
Population density in those counties was 6 times higher than in inland counties.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.660
8 of the 10 costliest hurricanes in the United States history have occurred since 2004.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.120
Those storms produced more than $200 billion in insured losses.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.240
causes.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.560
Because rates did not fully reflect long-term risk, the program repeatedly borrowed from

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:47.960
the Treasury to pay claims.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.420
A pool that was supposed to support itself became a recurring fiscal obligation.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.400
Local governments gained tax revenue from new coastal construction.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.160
The federal government kept the residual loss.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.500
Picture a builder the private market had already refused.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.680
That builder could now get coverage at a fraction of the true expected loss.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.340
The gap in price raised the capitalized value of waterfront parcels. Mortgage capital

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:17.140
that might have looked for safer sites followed the higher value instead. Even where coastal

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.840
permits did not rise, affordable insurance made marginal floodplain projects viable.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.260
Private capital alone would have rejected those projects.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:31.160
Decade after decade, the loop concentrated taxable value inside zones whose maps were drawn

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:36.380
on historical data, not on future climate. Each new structure added immediate local

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:41.500
revenue, and an incremental share of the national lost tail the Treasury ultimately absorbed.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.060
The original design had meant to replace ad hoc bailouts.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.900
What it also did was lower the private cost of locating homes and businesses in high

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.640
hazard zones, and shift future losses onto the public treasury.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.300
Repeated borrowing led Congress to authorize debt forgiveness.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:03.860
Reform efforts in 2012 and 2014 aimed at raising rates toward actuarial levels.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.300
Those increases met resistance from existing property owners and were later scaled back.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.940
Owners who had bought under the old pricing argued they had relied on the federal commitment.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.800
The initial subsidy had created expectations that later adjustments had to navigate.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.400
The right-your-own partnership with private insurers expanded.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.180
The core tension did not.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.780
Subsidized coverage and development incentives were still pulling in opposite directions.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:34.760
As of recent counts, the program serves more than 22,600 communities and works with

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.540
over 47 private carriers.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.760
Cumulative debt and exposure in high-risk zones continue to grow.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.000
No comprehensive reversal of the original subsidy structure has occurred.

00:05:46.005 --> 00:05:50.945
When Congress tied insurance availability to community participation without making premiums

00:05:50.945 --> 00:05:55.825
matched mapped risk, it rewarded more building where private markets had already called the

00:05:55.825 --> 00:05:56.845
hazard uninsurable.

00:05:56.845 --> 00:06:00.345
available. The thing being rewarded was location, not caution.

00:06:01.005 --> 00:06:06.485
Later statutes added mandatory purchase, but only for federally backed loans. Cash

00:06:06.485 --> 00:06:11.085
buyers and non-federal lenders still met the same cheap coverage, without that purchase

00:06:11.085 --> 00:06:16.565
rule as a break. A standard on the books is not the price a builder actually pays. If the

00:06:16.565 --> 00:06:21.925
premium is a fraction of expected loss, the parcel is worth more, and capital follows the gap.

00:06:21.925 --> 00:06:27.005
app. Watch who books the upside. A town can collect the new tax base while the national

00:06:27.005 --> 00:06:32.225
treasury holds the lost tail. That split is durable because each side is responding to

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:37.305
a number that looks rational on its own ledger. The same split between private refusal and

00:06:37.305 --> 00:06:41.905
a public backstop is the investing question modern investing techniques is built to sit

00:06:41.905 --> 00:06:44.925
with. If a government offers coverage after private

00:06:44.925 --> 00:06:49.765
insurers have already walked away from a place, what would you watch first whether the price

00:06:49.765 --> 00:06:54.425
still makes the risky sight cheaper than a safer one? That's the case for today. The

